Z||000000||zebrex||03-15-2000||11:04 PM||||I need to set up a web directory for my self at work so I cant send any pics yet but ive got a open aluminum or steel tube sticking up behind the firewall just to the right of my airbox . its about 3/4 or 1 ID and its wide open am I missing some kind of hose or emissions device? is it even important ? IF someone can post pics for me I can email them. <BR>                          Thanks<BR>                            Trent (Zebrex)<BR>I new I forgot something Its an 88 formula 2.8 auto<BR>[This message has been edited by zebrex (edited 03-15-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by zebrex (edited 03-15-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||03-15-2000||11:37 PM||||   Zebrex, That tube may be the remenant of some recall work done to your car. There used to be a tube that ran from the intake duct to the forward valve cover near the frwd fire wall. The tube was rerouted from the valve cover to the intake duct just in front of the throtlebody(steel tube). Also there was a heat shield installed on the forward manifold. This was done to prevent engine fires do to lack of adiquate ventillation and excessive heat from the exhaust manifold. Like I said this "MAY" be that tube. The other end of the tube should be farther right near the valve cover. Let us know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>  BAM-BAM, OUT <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000002||Shiner||03-16-2000||12:25 AM||||Are there two of said tubes, and they are kind of curvy, with no apparent use?  These are there to give some air to the ignition coil and the alternator (I belive).  I think these were a recall item on earlier V6 cars, if not all of them.  When I first saw them I went "WTF is that?", until I found out here on our great forum.  Somebody correct me if I am wrong or missing anything here.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-16-2000||01:41 AM||||You say you have a V-6. It is either the recall tube or it could be one of the cooling tubes. There are 4 outlets from a blower motor located right hand side of your trunk under the carpet. This blower motor blows air thru certain tubes to aid in cooling.||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||03-16-2000||11:23 AM||||Friends,<P>First and foremost, I was under the assumption that only the 85's had the cooling tubes.  This assumption could be totally misconstrued, but this is what I remember so please someone with another year verify the existance of these on your other year Fiero's.  <P>Also the two most prominent cooling tubes should end very near the alternator and ignition module respectively.  They are about 3/4" OD tubes though.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000005||wkayl||03-16-2000||11:30 AM||||FYI<BR>Both my son's and my 86GTs have the cooling tubes near the alt and ing coil.  In reference to the tube on the firewall, mine is open but on my son's car is hooked to the bottom of the plenum.  Does that meen that my son's car may not have had the recall?||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||03-16-2000||12:32 PM||||William,  Both my 86SE and 86GT (both V6) have the two cooling tubes on the trunk side.<P>zebrex, The tube on the fire wall is open because of the recall work.  I found this out this week because my 86SE (well maintained by previous owner) had the recall work done, and my 86GT (totally abused by previous owner) did not have the recall work done and still has that tube connected.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||03-16-2000||02:11 PM||||All V-6 Fiero's have the cooling tubes and the blower motor. As for the recall tube I know the recall removes the hose and leaves the metal tube.||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||03-16-2000||05:35 PM||||Gentlemen,<P>THX for the information on the tubes.  That factoid is now permanently etched in my brain.<P>Thanks Again,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000009||DRH||03-16-2000||06:36 PM||||My 88 Formula DOES NOT have the cooling tubes on the trunk side of the engine. The tubes and the corresponding fan are not mentioned in the 88 Pontiac manual. My understanding is they were eliminated in 88 and some heat shielding was added.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||WKDFIRO||03-16-2000||06:44 PM||||I can confirm that!<P>In 88 the "Baked Potato" exhaust came online for the 88 V6 making the blower tubes unnecessary. Drawback: The 88s are the only ones with crossover pipes that crack (with regularity). One theory is that condensation is trapped inside the heat shield and degrades the metal. I suppose its like wrapping your exhaust if its not stainless steel.||||reg||2
Z||000011||BAM-BAM||03-16-2000||08:39 PM||||   Zebrex, check out the post from 03-15-00, entitled V6 tube on valve cover. This post was written by Mshill. The reply  by DRH I believe might answert your question <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>  Be Cool! BAM Out!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000012||Oreif||03-17-2000||02:31 AM||||I stand corrected. I guess 88's didn't have the blower and tubes. I know 85 thru 87 did for sure. ||||reg||1
